    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d260">

                <pb facs="0353" n="343" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg343"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/7–648: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-07-06T18:00:00+09:00">July 6, 1948—6 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 6—10:51 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1235. I raised with Gimo question of Government educating Chinese people on
                    Soviet violations of the Sino-Soviet pact<anchor n="76" xml:id="d260-f1"/> and
                    behavior of Russian troops in Manchuria. Confirming what Vice Minister George
                    Yeh told us, as reported Embtel 632, April 8, Gimo replied <pb facs="0354"
                        n="344" xml:id="pg344"/> that action along lines suggested depended upon
                    American policy. When China could count on assistance from US in resisting
                    Soviet penetration, he was ready to abandon present timid policy and come out
                    openly against these violations of the pact and other general unfriendly
                    activities of Soviets.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d260-f1" n="76" xml:id="d260-f1-content">
                <p>Signed at Moscow, August 14, 1945; <hi rendition="#italic">United States
                        Relations With China</hi>, p. 585, or United Nations Treaty Series, vol. 10,
                    p. 300.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
